| Feature               | G1GC        | ZGC      | Shenandoah |
| --------------------- | ----------- | -------- | ---------- |
| Generational          | ✔ (logical) | ❌       | ❌         |
| Regions               | ✔           | ✔        | ✔          |
| STW pauses            | 10–200 ms   | ~1–10 ms | ~1–10 ms    |
| Concurrent compaction | ❌           | ✔        | ✔         |
| Large heap support    | ✔           | ✔✔       | ✔          |
| Default in J17        | ✔           | ❌        | ❌         |

======
G1GC: “Predictable pauses using region selection”
ZGC: “Move objects while the app is running using colored pointers”
Shenandoah: “Move objects while the app is running using forwarding pointers”
====

Big Picture First (important)
====================================
ZGC and Shenandoah are region-based but NOT classic generational collectors.
They aim for ultra-low pause times by doing almost everything concurrently.

Practical takeaway for you (Java 17):
====================================
In Java 17, ZGC is non-generational, so you should not think in Eden/S0/S1/Old.
In Java 21+, ZGC gets a generational mode that makes it more competitive for typical “lots of short-lived objects” services.
1️⃣ ZGC (Java 17)
=========================
Heap Model
==========
Heap
 ├── ZRegion
 ├── ZRegion
 ├── ZRegion
 └── ...
Fixed-size regions called ZPages
No young / old split
Objects can be relocated while application threads run

Key innovation: Colored Pointers
===============================
ZGC encodes GC state inside object pointers:
[ metadata | address ]
Used for:
    Marked?
    Relocating?
    Remapped?
➡️ Application threads help the GC by fixing pointers on the fly.

GC Phases (simplified)
===========
Concurrent Mark
Concurrent Relocate
Concurrent Remap
Tiny STW pauses only for:
    Root processing
    Metadata setup
⏱ Typical pause: sub-10ms, even with large heaps (100s of GB)

When ZGC shines
    Large heaps
    Latency-sensitive systems
    Spiky allocation rates
    Cloud / container workloads
-XX:+UseZGC
-XX:MaxGCPauseMillis=10

ZGC starts to make sense when your heap is big enough that G1’s worst-case pauses
become noticeable and you care about tail latency.

Here are useful, real-world heuristics:

Practical size bands
≤ 2–4 GB heap
    Usually not worth ZGC
    G1 is typically simpler and more CPU-efficient
    Any pauses are usually already small if the heap is small

~4–16 GB heap

    Borderline
    Choose ZGC if you have strict p99/p999 latency SLOs (interactive API, trading path)
    Otherwise stick to G1 for cost/throughput

~16–64 GB heap
    This is where ZGC commonly shines
    G1 can still be fine, but you start seeing:
    longer / spikier pauses under pressure
    mixed GC cycles that sometimes bite latency
    ZGC tends to keep pauses consistently low

64 GB+ heap
    Strong ZGC territory (if latency matters)
    This is the classic “ZGC value prop” zone: very large heaps with very small pauses
    And yes, ZGC can work with hundreds of GB / TB-class heaps, but whether you should depends on CPU + memory headroom and workload.

2️⃣ Shenandoah (Java 17)
=========================
Heap model
Heap
 ├── Region
 ├── Region
 ├── Region
 └── ...

Regions similar to G1
No generational separation in Java 17
Uses forwarding pointers, not colored pointers

Key innovation: Brooks Pointer
-------------------------------------
Each object has an extra indirection pointer:
object -> forwarding pointer -> actual object

Allows:
    Concurrent evacuation
    Concurrent compaction
    Zero long STW pauses
Trade-off:
    Slight memory overhead per object

GC Phases
    Concurrent Mark
    Concurrent Evacuation
    Concurrent Update References
    Very short STW pauses only for coordination

When Shenandoah shines
    Latency-critical services
    Predictable pauses
    Medium-to-large heaps

Red Hat / OpenShift environments

Shenandoah flags (Java 17)
-XX:+UseShenandoahGC

Optional:

-XX:MaxGCPauseMillis=10

